\subsection{Attempt: how much goal elicitation can exact matching require?}
The source does not define learner goals or a mapping from tasks to them. In a deliberately finite model, assume exactly one of $m\ge2$ declared goals applies, each requiring a different task framing. A learner can answer truthful binary membership questions about this set of goals. I tested whether exact individual matching can avoid elicitation altogether.
\begin{proposition}
Every deterministic procedure guaranteed to select the right framing for all $m$ goals requires at least $\lceil\log_2m\rceil$ binary answers in the worst case. This bound is achievable when arbitrary goal-subset queries are allowed.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A procedure using at most $h$ binary answers is a binary decision tree of depth at most $h$, with at most $2^h$ leaves. Two different goals cannot terminate at the same leaf, because the output framing there is fixed and correct for at most one of them. Thus $2^h\ge m$. For achievability, assign each goal a distinct binary string of length $\lceil\log_2m\rceil$ and ask, for each coordinate, whether the goal belongs to the subset whose string has a one in that coordinate. The answer string identifies the goal and hence its prescribed framing.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
